    "diff_hunk": "@@ -102731,6 +102754,33 @@ dictionary <dfn>StorageEventInit</dfn> : <span>EventInit</span> {\n   <p>To handle these cases, parsers have a <dfn>script nesting level</dfn>, which must be initially\n   set to zero, and a <dfn>parser pause flag</dfn>, which must be initially set to false.</p>\n \n+  <div class=\"XXX\">\n+   <p>The tree constructor stage modifies the DOM, which can only be modified from the event loop,\n+   yet that stage must run in parallel. While this is sorted out, implementations may act in one of\n+   two ways:</p>\n+\n+   <ul>\n+    <li><p>Act as if <span data-x=\"I/O queue push\">pushing</span> an item onto the <span>input byte\n+    stream</span> or onto the <span>input stream</span> would instead run the corresponding steps of\n+    the parser immediately. Under this model, the parser's reentrant behavior would work exactly as\n+    expected from the remainder of the spec.</p></li>\n+\n+    <li>\n+     <p>Act as if the tree constructor stage queued tasks on the event loop that cause the\n+     corresponding modifications on the DOM. In order to prevent user code from observing states of\n+     the DOM that couldn't be observed from the single-threaded model, no more than one task can be\n+     queued during the processing of each token. Additionally, if the <span>insertion point</span>\n+     is just before the <span>next input character</span>, consuming it must block until one or more\n+     tokens are inserted before it, so that data coming in from the network is only processed after\n+     script-inserted data (or alternatively, perform speculative parsing).</p>\n+\n+     <p>In this model, most of the processing for the <a href=\"#scriptEndTag\"><code>script</code>\n+     end tag</a> would take place on the event loop and, and so calling <code",
